Now after typing "l", assertion expects text "#autoComplete_result_0" = "Blackberry". Wait they expect the first item (index 0) to be Blackberry. That suggests after typing "l", the list is sorted or filtered such that Blackberry is first. Let's see ordering: The data source order is as given. Filtering for "bl" yields: Bilberry, Blackberry, Blackcurrant, Blueberry, Boysenberry. In that order, first is Bilberry, not Blackberry. So why would Blackberry be first? Perhaps the search engine is "strict" meaning it matches only if the query matches the start of the word? Actually "strict" maybe means match whole word? Or maybe they use some sorting? Not sure.

Alternatively maybe after typing "l", they also pressed ArrowDown? Wait step 5 is type "l" with assertion about #autoComplete_result_0 text = Blackberry. Step 6 is ArrowDown press with assertion that aria-selected on #autoComplete_result_0 = true. So after typing "l", they expect the first item to be Blackberry (so that ArrowDown selects it? Actually ArrowDown would move selection down; but they assert that after ArrowDown, the first item becomes selected. That implies before ArrowDown, nothing is selected (or maybe selection is -1). After ArrowDown, selection goes to index 0. So they expect that after typing "l", no item is selected (aria-activedescendant maybe not set) and pressing ArrowDown selects the first item.

